WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.059
- I'm just not going to
work that hard tonight.

00:00:02.059 --> 00:00:04.827
This time, you may have
gotten too close to the edge.

00:00:04.827 --> 00:00:07.624
Can we actually spot some
of those innovative ideas?

00:00:07.624 --> 00:00:09.609
The only time the the
success comes before work

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:10.804
is in the dictionary.

00:00:10.804 --> 00:00:12.320
Working with smart people

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.234
means that you'll be
challenged to do your best.

00:00:14.234 --> 00:00:17.395
Technology companies
live and die by talent.

00:00:17.395 --> 00:00:20.158
We want them to do the
three things twice as well

00:00:20.158 --> 00:00:21.954
or do we want to do twice as many things?

00:00:21.954 --> 00:00:23.588
Be an information fountain.

00:00:23.588 --> 00:00:24.934
Are you on a trajectory for success

00:00:24.934 --> 00:00:26.806
or are you on trajectory for failure?

00:00:26.806 --> 00:00:29.927
And we basically did each of
those in a really small scale.

00:00:29.927 --> 00:00:32.274
- She's an American
businesswoman and the president

00:00:32.274 --> 00:00:34.096
and CEO of Yahoo.

00:00:34.096 --> 00:00:36.176
Previously she was a longtime executive

00:00:36.176 --> 00:00:38.476
and key spokesperson for Google.

00:00:38.476 --> 00:00:42.820
Forbes estimates her net
worth to be $380 million.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:44.531
She's Marisa Mayer and here are

00:00:44.531 --> 00:00:46.737
her top 10 rules for success.

00:00:46.737 --> 00:00:49.736
(swooping, ball bouncing)

00:00:49.736 --> 00:00:53.076
- I've always had a
philosophy around balance.

00:00:53.076 --> 00:00:54.430
A lot of people talk about balance,

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:55.701
I don't really believe in balance.

00:00:55.701 --> 00:00:58.219
But I do believe in finding your rhythm.

00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:01.065
And I think a lot of it has to do not with

00:01:01.065 --> 00:01:03.422
three square meals a day,
time at home at night,

00:01:03.422 --> 00:01:06.786
eight hours of sleep, I think
it has to do with resentment.

00:01:06.786 --> 00:01:09.785
Because I think what fuels
burnout is this notion of

00:01:09.785 --> 00:01:12.782
well wait I sacrificed this thing,

00:01:12.782 --> 00:01:14.417
and it honestly meant too much to me

00:01:14.417 --> 00:01:18.578
and it made me resentful
and for me it's really about

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:22.696
being able to walk out of
the office for one week every

00:01:22.696 --> 00:01:26.161
four to six months, find
if I start pushing that out

00:01:26.161 --> 00:01:28.428
or delaying vacations
I'll be like you know,

00:01:28.428 --> 00:01:30.013
I'm just not going to
work that hard tonight.

00:01:30.013 --> 00:01:32.231
I didn't get to take that
trip I really wanted to take.

00:01:32.231 --> 00:01:33.955
So I think you can work arbitrarily hard

00:01:33.955 --> 00:01:35.048
for really hard periods of time,

00:01:35.048 --> 00:01:36.820
but you need to know yourself well enough

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:39.538
that you can avoid
resentment and avoid burnout.

00:01:39.538 --> 00:01:41.935
Doing something you aren't
ready to isn't comfortable.

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:44.646
For me, and I assume for
many of you it gives you that

00:01:44.646 --> 00:01:47.007
uneasy upset feeling in your stomach,

00:01:47.007 --> 00:01:49.323
that sense that this
time you may have gotten

00:01:49.323 --> 00:01:51.224
too close to the edge.

00:01:51.224 --> 00:01:53.021
But in pushing through that discomfort,

00:01:53.021 --> 00:01:55.619
you'll learn a lot more about yourself.

00:01:55.619 --> 00:01:58.554
You'll learn to do something
you didn't think you could do.

00:01:58.554 --> 00:02:00.528
Or you'll learn where your limits are.

00:02:00.528 --> 00:02:02.705
Either is valuable.

00:02:02.705 --> 00:02:04.992
It's important to push
through that uneasiness though

00:02:04.992 --> 00:02:07.162
because in that moment
of finding your courage

00:02:07.162 --> 00:02:09.892
you really grow and you really reach.

00:02:09.892 --> 00:02:13.720
I'll say it again, do something
you're not ready to do.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.545
- There's a natural conflict isn't there

00:02:15.545 --> 00:02:20.221
in the world of technology
between innovation and execution?

00:02:20.221 --> 00:02:22.465
We see many companies struggling with it.

00:02:22.465 --> 00:02:24.651
Can both be done well at the same time?

00:02:24.651 --> 00:02:26.146
- It's funny because it
was pointed out to me

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:30.469
a few years ago that one
hypothesis is that innovation,

00:02:30.469 --> 00:02:32.351
if you think about, what's
the opposite of innovation?

00:02:32.351 --> 00:02:34.654
A lot of people would say
well it's the status quo,

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:36.122
it's stagnancy.

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:38.784
And there's another school
of thought which says that

00:02:38.784 --> 00:02:41.358
the opposite of innovation is execution.

00:02:41.358 --> 00:02:44.293
That if you have to be in
heads down execution mode,

00:02:44.293 --> 00:02:46.731
it's very hard to find
the space to innovate,

00:02:46.731 --> 00:02:49.517
to have those new ideas
and to pull things in.

00:02:49.517 --> 00:02:51.738
And I know that for us at Yahoo right now,

00:02:51.738 --> 00:02:55.320
this is going to be a
great period of execution.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.068
Can we take these
products that we have and

00:02:58.068 --> 00:02:59.759
revitalize them for the web?

00:02:59.759 --> 00:03:02.455
And also make the transition to mobile?

00:03:02.455 --> 00:03:04.038
And while we're doing that execution,

00:03:04.038 --> 00:03:05.487
will there be room to innovate?

00:03:05.487 --> 00:03:09.282
To say hey, this is how Yahoo
Groups worked on the web,

00:03:09.282 --> 00:03:11.568
but now there's all these
new opportunities in terms of

00:03:11.568 --> 00:03:14.158
how group communication
should work on the phone.

00:03:14.158 --> 00:03:17.444
Can we actually spot some
of those innovative ideas?

00:03:17.444 --> 00:03:19.703
And I think there is, but it's hard

00:03:19.703 --> 00:03:22.913
'cause I do think that
they are natural opposites.

00:03:22.913 --> 00:03:26.034
- Is size a barrier to innovation?

00:03:26.034 --> 00:03:30.130
- I don't think so because
I think you can innovate

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:32.568
at scale and with large size,

00:03:32.568 --> 00:03:35.071
but you have to be very
principled about it.

00:03:35.071 --> 00:03:38.286
You know if you have,
say, 10 engineers and

00:03:38.286 --> 00:03:41.490
you're going to grow that to be 20 or 30,

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.108
do you want to be doing
the same set of things

00:03:44.108 --> 00:03:45.846
two or three times better?

00:03:45.846 --> 00:03:49.244
Or do you want to be doing two
or three times more things?

00:03:49.244 --> 00:03:51.517
And in terms of really, interestingly,

00:03:51.517 --> 00:03:53.022
in terms of execution,
this is a great example

00:03:53.022 --> 00:03:56.291
of why they're so opposite,
so much opposites that

00:03:56.291 --> 00:03:58.631
if you really wanted to execute perfectly,

00:03:58.631 --> 00:04:00.687
get the design exactly
right, really work though

00:04:00.687 --> 00:04:03.816
all the details, you didn't
ask two to three times

00:04:03.816 --> 00:04:05.602
as many people per project.

00:04:05.602 --> 00:04:07.384
If you want to find those new ideas,

00:04:07.384 --> 00:04:09.115
those far flung ideas,
those things you might not

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:12.057
otherwise find, you want
to take those same people

00:04:12.057 --> 00:04:13.536
and put them on something that's far flung

00:04:13.536 --> 00:04:15.266
that you've never thought about.

00:04:15.266 --> 00:04:18.819
And so it really is that
there is this tension.

00:04:18.819 --> 00:04:20.923
So I think that you can innovate at scale,

00:04:20.923 --> 00:04:23.882
but you need to save
room to have small teams

00:04:23.882 --> 00:04:26.157
working on those far flung ideas.

00:04:26.157 --> 00:04:29.657
- What's the most
important thing that you do

00:04:29.657 --> 00:04:31.240
to get it all done?

00:04:32.649 --> 00:04:33.787
- Hmm.

00:04:33.787 --> 00:04:36.478
You have to ruthlessly prioritize.

00:04:36.478 --> 00:04:38.263
And I do, and that's one of the reasons

00:04:38.263 --> 00:04:40.696
I haven't been talking
and I'm going to go back

00:04:40.696 --> 00:04:43.801
to not talking for a while after tonight

00:04:43.801 --> 00:04:46.408
because I've been really
internally focused.

00:04:46.408 --> 00:04:48.775
You know I'm from Wisconsin and

00:04:48.775 --> 00:04:50.937
in Wisconsin you know there's
all kinds of different people

00:04:50.937 --> 00:04:52.347
but the one thing that's true of all of us

00:04:52.347 --> 00:04:55.910
is we all follow the Green Bay
Packers to different degrees.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:56.743
And

00:04:57.804 --> 00:04:59.981
the Green Bay Packers, the
legacy and the lore there

00:04:59.981 --> 00:05:01.842
comes from their first
coach Vince Lombardi

00:05:01.842 --> 00:05:04.222
who coached them to their
early Super Bowl wins.

00:05:04.222 --> 00:05:06.124
And he's actually quite a pundit.

00:05:06.124 --> 00:05:08.211
It turns out these kind
of phrases you hear

00:05:08.211 --> 00:05:11.074
like "quitters never win
and winners never quit,"

00:05:11.074 --> 00:05:12.481
that's a Lombardi quote.

00:05:12.481 --> 00:05:15.423
Right, now "the only time
success comes before work

00:05:15.423 --> 00:05:18.736
is in the dictionary," that's
a Lombardi quote, right?

00:05:18.736 --> 00:05:22.596
And Vince Lombardi says "in my
life there are three things,

00:05:22.596 --> 00:05:27.359
"God, family, and the Green
Bay Packers, in that order."

00:05:27.359 --> 00:05:28.784
Right? (laughing)

00:05:28.784 --> 00:05:32.342
And I think that for me
it's God, family and Yahoo

00:05:32.342 --> 00:05:33.709
in that order.

00:05:33.709 --> 00:05:35.676
Find the smartest people you can

00:05:35.676 --> 00:05:37.683
and surround yourself with them.

00:05:37.683 --> 00:05:39.548
Working with smart people means

00:05:39.548 --> 00:05:42.209
that you'll be challenged to do your best.

00:05:42.209 --> 00:05:44.030
You'll have to strive
to keep up with them,

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:47.045
and as a result, they will
elevate your thinking.

00:05:47.045 --> 00:05:50.993
When there are better players
around you, you get better.

00:05:50.993 --> 00:05:53.204
I first noticed this
when I was growing up.

00:05:53.204 --> 00:05:55.278
I had a friend, Laura Beckman.

00:05:55.278 --> 00:05:57.423
Laura tried out for the volleyball team

00:05:57.423 --> 00:05:59.138
her junior year of high school,

00:05:59.138 --> 00:06:02.549
and at the end of the tryouts
was given a hard choice.

00:06:02.549 --> 00:06:05.216
Bench on varsity or start on JV.

00:06:06.404 --> 00:06:08.281
Most people when they're
faced with this choice

00:06:08.281 --> 00:06:10.642
choose to play, I will pick JV.

00:06:10.642 --> 00:06:14.506
Laura did the opposite, she chose varsity.

00:06:14.506 --> 00:06:16.992
And she benched the whole season.

00:06:16.992 --> 00:06:19.382
But then an amazing thing happened.

00:06:19.382 --> 00:06:22.625
Senior year when she tried
out, she made varsity

00:06:22.625 --> 00:06:25.193
as a starter, and all the JV starters

00:06:25.193 --> 00:06:27.280
from the previous year benched.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:29.562
And I remember asking
Laura, how did you know

00:06:29.562 --> 00:06:30.840
to choose varsity?

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:33.098
And she said, I just knew.

00:06:33.098 --> 00:06:35.856
I just knew if I got to
practice with the better players

00:06:35.856 --> 00:06:38.359
every day I would become a better player

00:06:38.359 --> 00:06:41.155
even if I didn't get to play in the games.

00:06:41.155 --> 00:06:44.267
How does this sporting example
relate to smart people?

00:06:44.267 --> 00:06:48.051
I deeply believe that the same
thing happens intellectually.

00:06:48.051 --> 00:06:50.590
When you surround
yourself with smart people

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:52.191
they challenge you to think harder

00:06:52.191 --> 00:06:54.312
and in entirely different ways.

00:06:54.312 --> 00:06:55.775
- What have you focused on?

00:06:55.775 --> 00:06:57.619
What are you most excited
about when it comes

00:06:57.619 --> 00:06:59.713
to innovative technologies,
particularly the ones

00:06:59.713 --> 00:07:02.512
you have some control over?

00:07:02.512 --> 00:07:06.105
- Well I was really overall just genuinely

00:07:06.105 --> 00:07:07.085
pleased and surprised.

00:07:07.085 --> 00:07:09.217
I knew that there had to
be great people at Yahoo.

00:07:09.217 --> 00:07:11.672
The same way that when
you sometimes look at art

00:07:11.672 --> 00:07:13.882
you can tell if it was created

00:07:13.882 --> 00:07:17.641
by a nice person or not or
a depressed person or not.

00:07:17.641 --> 00:07:19.514
And when you feel Yahoo's
products you can tell

00:07:19.514 --> 00:07:21.445
that there's really nice, very smart

00:07:21.445 --> 00:07:23.789
competent people there
that have a great time.

00:07:23.789 --> 00:07:27.289
And it's true, it's a
great company overall

00:07:27.289 --> 00:07:29.299
that has a very fun culture.

00:07:29.299 --> 00:07:32.889
And for my first few months
my focus really was on people.

00:07:32.889 --> 00:07:36.223
Because I believe fundamentally that

00:07:36.223 --> 00:07:39.718
technology companies
live and die by talent

00:07:39.718 --> 00:07:43.058
and that's why when people
talk about the talent wars,

00:07:43.058 --> 00:07:44.485
it's not really that some of the companies

00:07:44.485 --> 00:07:46.016
that are in the talent
wars are that competitive

00:07:46.016 --> 00:07:48.428
with each other, it's just
that when you start to see

00:07:48.428 --> 00:07:51.452
the best people migrating
from one company to the next,

00:07:51.452 --> 00:07:53.684
it means then that the
next wave is starting.

00:07:53.684 --> 00:07:55.760
And so I got very focused on people,

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:58.337
building the right team,
particularly the executive layer

00:07:58.337 --> 00:08:00.181
but all through the business.

00:08:00.181 --> 00:08:02.652
And also the overall environment.

00:08:02.652 --> 00:08:04.822
And part of that was because
I wanted to make sure that

00:08:04.822 --> 00:08:07.023
Yahoo was absolutely
the best place to work

00:08:07.023 --> 00:08:09.544
and the people really wanted
to come and work there

00:08:09.544 --> 00:08:11.608
because that will help
with the talent piece.

00:08:11.608 --> 00:08:15.826
But also because I believe
that really strong companies

00:08:15.826 --> 00:08:18.466
all have very strong cultures.

00:08:18.466 --> 00:08:20.857
And Yahoo's no exception, it's
been a very strong company

00:08:20.857 --> 00:08:23.470
for a very long time,
it's got a strong culture.

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:26.562
It's different from every
other corporate culture.

00:08:26.562 --> 00:08:29.006
Each has their own unique
and individual flavor.

00:08:29.006 --> 00:08:31.674
And I really wanted to
find a way to amplify it.

00:08:31.674 --> 00:08:34.907
Because in amplifying it,
that's how you find the energy.

00:08:34.907 --> 00:08:37.704
And the energy is what you can
harness into that innovation

00:08:37.704 --> 00:08:41.262
and say, OK, if we have people
and they are really excited

00:08:41.262 --> 00:08:42.531
about what they're working on every day

00:08:42.531 --> 00:08:45.993
and they realize that the
next big hurdle is mobile,

00:08:45.993 --> 00:08:48.391
you can take that energy
around the culture

00:08:48.391 --> 00:08:50.748
and find fun ways to apply it

00:08:50.748 --> 00:08:52.844
that can be really
impactful for end users.

00:08:52.844 --> 00:08:55.238
We like to work with really small teams.

00:08:55.238 --> 00:08:57.550
When I started there
were about nine engineers

00:08:57.550 --> 00:08:59.562
and Larry and Sergey said
well, we have nine engineers

00:08:59.562 --> 00:09:01.752
and right now they do about
three different things.

00:09:01.752 --> 00:09:03.499
So if we double and we have 18 engineers

00:09:03.499 --> 00:09:06.311
would we want them to do the
three things twice as well?

00:09:06.311 --> 00:09:08.464
Or do we want to do twice as many things?

00:09:08.464 --> 00:09:09.836
And they said well
obviously we'd want to do

00:09:09.836 --> 00:09:11.346
twice as many things. (laughing)

00:09:11.346 --> 00:09:13.347
And so that's how we've grown.

00:09:13.347 --> 00:09:14.877
We've tried to keep the teams really small

00:09:14.877 --> 00:09:16.686
which leads to a sense of empowerment,

00:09:16.686 --> 00:09:18.680
people making decisions,

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:20.246
around what's the best feature?

00:09:20.246 --> 00:09:21.433
What do their users need?

00:09:21.433 --> 00:09:23.300
How are they going to
build the best product?

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:26.057
And it allows also for
them to be really agile.

00:09:26.057 --> 00:09:28.771
We try and avoid meetings
and like a lot of reasons,

00:09:28.771 --> 00:09:30.786
one of the great things that
happens with a small team

00:09:30.786 --> 00:09:32.427
is you can put them
all in the same office.

00:09:32.427 --> 00:09:34.379
At Google we usually
have three or four people

00:09:34.379 --> 00:09:37.960
in each office and that
works really well because

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:39.229
when they wanted to make a decision

00:09:39.229 --> 00:09:41.398
people just roll back from
their desk and say hey!

00:09:41.398 --> 00:09:43.175
- [Charlie] Turn around
go what about this?

00:09:43.175 --> 00:09:44.656
- I've got something that
we need to decide right now.

00:09:44.656 --> 00:09:48.505
So that allows the teams
to really be very agile.

00:09:48.505 --> 00:09:50.718
Be an information fountain.

00:09:50.718 --> 00:09:53.944
Information used to be
scarce and expensive

00:09:53.944 --> 00:09:57.244
and power came through
hoarding it and brokering it.

00:09:57.244 --> 00:09:59.102
Today it's just the opposite.

00:09:59.102 --> 00:10:01.576
Power comes from sharing information.

00:10:01.576 --> 00:10:03.583
Tell everyone everything.

00:10:03.583 --> 00:10:05.702
The more valuable the better.

00:10:05.702 --> 00:10:07.533
Sharing leads to connections,

00:10:07.533 --> 00:10:10.116
connections lead to collaboration,

00:10:10.116 --> 00:10:13.458
collaboration leads to
creativity and innovation.

00:10:13.458 --> 00:10:17.448
Creativity and innovation
are what change the world.

00:10:17.448 --> 00:10:20.089
Lack of sharing can kill
innovation in big companies

00:10:20.089 --> 00:10:23.706
where divisions turn into
information fortresses.

00:10:23.706 --> 00:10:26.079
They compete with each other
and end up guarding information

00:10:26.079 --> 00:10:29.574
from each other as zealously
as they do from competitors.

00:10:29.574 --> 00:10:31.570
Everyone loses as a result.

00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:34.742
Help others find their way
by sharing the information

00:10:34.742 --> 00:10:36.831
and ideas you have.

00:10:36.831 --> 00:10:38.757
- I was wondering if you
could talk a little bit

00:10:38.757 --> 00:10:41.931
about how failure sits in the middle of

00:10:41.931 --> 00:10:44.088
startups and entrepreneur.

00:10:44.088 --> 00:10:46.477
- Well failure is a fact of life.

00:10:46.477 --> 00:10:47.632
The most important thing of course

00:10:47.632 --> 00:10:49.002
is to learn from failure.

00:10:49.002 --> 00:10:51.272
But I also think that the other thing

00:10:51.272 --> 00:10:52.710
that people don't realize about failure

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:56.547
is that it's totally fine
as long as you do it fast.

00:10:56.547 --> 00:10:57.496
(laughing)
Right?

00:10:57.496 --> 00:10:59.733
So if you fail fast and then
you're on to the next thing

00:10:59.733 --> 00:11:02.349
that's actually successful,
you're much better off.

00:11:02.349 --> 00:11:05.430
And so you've got to be
constantly monitoring your effort,

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:07.770
figuring out are you on
a trajectory for success?

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:09.850
Or are you on a trajectory for failure?

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:11.972
And if you're on a trajectory for failure,

00:11:11.972 --> 00:11:14.424
decide to fail fast, give it up,

00:11:14.424 --> 00:11:16.555
and move on to the next
thing that can be successful.

00:11:16.555 --> 00:11:18.555
To me, 2014 was our time

00:11:19.408 --> 00:11:20.993
to do a lot of different experiments.

00:11:20.993 --> 00:11:22.932
We experimented with some partner content,

00:11:22.932 --> 00:11:26.053
we experimented with some
live content with Live Nation.

00:11:26.053 --> 00:11:29.043
We had a lot of our editors
in different verticals

00:11:29.043 --> 00:11:31.286
work on their own series and clips

00:11:31.286 --> 00:11:32.970
of what they wanted to work on.

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:34.605
We did some original content.

00:11:34.605 --> 00:11:37.539
And we basically did each of
those at a really small scale

00:11:37.539 --> 00:11:39.479
to understand what worked well,

00:11:39.479 --> 00:11:41.172
what did we think we
produced with quality?

00:11:41.172 --> 00:11:43.044
What did our audience respond to?

00:11:43.044 --> 00:11:45.952
And now in 2015 returning
to it we're really looking

00:11:45.952 --> 00:11:48.358
and saying OK, what we
saw work really well

00:11:48.358 --> 00:11:50.389
was the magazine content,

00:11:50.389 --> 00:11:52.642
the deep verticals that
Yahoo's always been known for.

00:11:52.642 --> 00:11:55.240
How can we build out more video there?

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:57.404
How can we work out more on content?

00:11:57.404 --> 00:11:59.531
So when we do things like The Ultimate DJ,

00:11:59.531 --> 00:12:01.277
we're going to be running
an American Idol-like show

00:12:01.277 --> 00:12:04.482
with Simon Cowell to find the
bet DJ in the world next year.

00:12:04.482 --> 00:12:06.409
That aligns really
deeply with Yahoo music.

00:12:06.409 --> 00:12:09.749
The NFL opportunity aligns
really deeply with Yahoo Sports.

00:12:09.749 --> 00:12:13.244
And so I think that it really
helped us shape our content

00:12:13.244 --> 00:12:16.216
experience to think about, let's
run a bunch of experiments,

00:12:16.216 --> 00:12:18.972
build a bunch of different
prototypes, see what works

00:12:18.972 --> 00:12:21.793
and what really our users respond to,

00:12:21.793 --> 00:12:24.158
and that's things like Live Nation

00:12:24.158 --> 00:12:26.472
and some of the verticals
we've always been strong in,

00:12:26.472 --> 00:12:29.034
and then how do we build even
more content in that vein.

00:12:29.034 --> 00:12:30.131
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:12:30.131 --> 00:12:33.228
I made this video because
Home Akaza asked me to.

00:12:33.228 --> 00:12:34.642
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:12:34.642 --> 00:12:36.275
that you want me to profile next,

00:12:36.275 --> 00:12:38.787
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:12:38.787 --> 00:12:41.214
I'd also love to know which
of Marissa's top ten rules

00:12:41.214 --> 00:12:43.606
meant the most to you and
had the biggest impact.

00:12:43.606 --> 00:12:45.823
Leave it in the comments and
I'll join in the discussion.

00:12:45.823 --> 00:12:47.044
Thank you so much for watching.

00:12:47.044 --> 00:12:49.985
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

00:12:49.985 --> 00:12:53.568
(swooshing, ball bouncing)

00:12:55.975 --> 00:12:58.470
- You described, a couple of months ago

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:02.483
how one of your employees asked how Yahoo

00:13:02.483 --> 00:13:04.566
is going to compete if it doesn't have one

00:13:04.566 --> 00:13:08.739
of these four key
distribution technologies.

00:13:08.739 --> 00:13:11.545
There's the mobile operating
system, mobile hardware,

00:13:11.545 --> 00:13:13.462
the browser and social.

00:13:15.529 --> 00:13:17.388
I don't know that we got an answer.

00:13:17.388 --> 00:13:19.534
- Yeah so it was funny
'cause one of our employees

00:13:19.534 --> 00:13:20.909
at one of the company meetings asked that

00:13:20.909 --> 00:13:22.827
and they said given that we
don't have mobile hardware,

00:13:22.827 --> 00:13:25.655
mobile OS, a browser or a social network,

00:13:25.655 --> 00:13:27.059
how are we going to compete?

00:13:27.059 --> 00:13:28.792
- That's not just a question for Yahoo,

00:13:28.792 --> 00:13:30.417
clearly it's a question for every company

00:13:30.417 --> 00:13:33.139
that seeks to compete with

00:13:33.139 --> 00:13:35.364
those others that have those
key enabling technologies.

00:13:35.364 --> 00:13:37.951
- Sure, and you know, of the
four horsemen of the internet

00:13:37.951 --> 00:13:40.901
to adopt that analogy, almost
all of them are playing

00:13:40.901 --> 00:13:44.428
in one if not several of those medium.

00:13:44.428 --> 00:13:47.208
But I think that the big piece here

00:13:47.208 --> 00:13:49.091
is that it really allows us to partner.

00:13:49.091 --> 00:13:50.832
Yahoo's always been a
very friendly company,

00:13:50.832 --> 00:13:53.092
has always been, because
of our focus also,

00:13:53.092 --> 00:13:55.733
in addition to technology
but also on media,

00:13:55.733 --> 00:13:58.017
it ultimately means that
there's really an opportunity

00:13:58.017 --> 00:14:00.430
for strong partnerships
and that's what we'll

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:02.779
be focused on so we work with,

00:14:02.779 --> 00:14:04.490
for example, Apple and Google.

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:06.563
In terms of the operating system,

00:14:06.563 --> 00:14:08.427
in terms of the social
network we have a strong

00:14:08.427 --> 00:14:10.553
partnership with Facebook.

00:14:10.553 --> 00:14:12.426
And so we're able to

00:14:12.426 --> 00:14:14.636
work with some of these players
that have a lot of strength

00:14:14.636 --> 00:14:17.668
in order to really bolster
our user experiences

00:14:17.668 --> 00:14:19.724
that we offer on the Yahoo site.

00:14:19.724 --> 00:14:23.153
My advice for women is
the same as it is for men

00:14:23.153 --> 00:14:24.389
which is that you should find something

00:14:24.389 --> 00:14:25.859
that you're really passionate about

00:14:25.859 --> 00:14:28.680
because passion is a
gender neutralizing force.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:31.077
- Lovely!
- And I really

00:14:31.077 --> 00:14:34.491
think that you should work for
someone who believes in you.

00:14:34.491 --> 00:14:36.709
Work for people who believe
in you and invest in you

00:14:36.709 --> 00:14:38.559
because that's how you'll
go far in your career.

00:14:38.559 --> 00:14:40.610
You're going to give
them a lot of your time,

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:43.315
be it your own startup, be it
a company that you're joining.

00:14:43.315 --> 00:14:47.424
And you want to make sure that you also

00:14:47.424 --> 00:14:49.369
get the responsibility and the fulfillment

00:14:49.369 --> 00:14:50.513
that you're looking for.

00:14:50.513 --> 00:14:51.837
And so those are my two words of advice.

00:14:51.837 --> 00:14:54.944
Find somewhere where
you're really passionate,

00:14:54.944 --> 00:14:56.992
find somewhere where
someone believes in you,

00:14:56.992 --> 00:14:58.671
and I guess my third piece of advice is

00:14:58.671 --> 00:15:00.702
find somewhere very comfortable.

00:15:00.702 --> 00:15:03.689
It's interesting for me
because I'm a very shy person

00:15:03.689 --> 00:15:04.929
by my nature

00:15:04.929 --> 00:15:07.907
and at Google people don't
believe I'm shy at all

00:15:07.907 --> 00:15:09.614
because I'm very comfortable there.

00:15:09.614 --> 00:15:11.060
And that I think is really important,

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:14.024
one of the most important
things for all entrepreneurs,

00:15:14.024 --> 00:15:16.237
especially for women
is to find your voice.

00:15:16.237 --> 00:15:18.331
And if you can't express
what you're thinking

00:15:18.331 --> 00:15:21.918
and what you believe
in, that's a real issue.

00:15:21.918 --> 00:15:24.665
And so I think it's really important for

00:15:24.665 --> 00:15:27.875
women entrepreneurs
especially to find somewhere

00:15:27.875 --> 00:15:29.110
where they're comfortable.

00:15:29.110 --> 00:15:30.963
The ads in email story is actually

00:15:30.963 --> 00:15:33.434
a really embarrassing story for me.

00:15:33.434 --> 00:15:34.880
In terms of how I almost blocked

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:37.185
a multi billion dollar business.

00:15:37.185 --> 00:15:40.351
(laughing) So it was
the early days of Gmail.

00:15:40.351 --> 00:15:43.141
I was the product manager,
my office mate, Paul Buchheit

00:15:43.141 --> 00:15:44.928
was the technical lead.

00:15:44.928 --> 00:15:46.978
And you know, you're
musing about a product,

00:15:46.978 --> 00:15:48.921
and you're building it,
and you're speccing it.

00:15:48.921 --> 00:15:50.244
But every now and then you just kind of

00:15:50.244 --> 00:15:51.578
need to take your mind off of it

00:15:51.578 --> 00:15:53.000
and brainstorm further in the future.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:55.365
And so one night Paul
and I were in our office

00:15:55.365 --> 00:15:57.161
and we always have
shared offices at Google

00:15:57.161 --> 00:16:00.303
so there's three or four
of us in there and Paul

00:16:00.303 --> 00:16:03.083
was like, how are we ever
going to make money from email?

00:16:03.083 --> 00:16:04.947
And I was like well Paul, this is simple,

00:16:04.947 --> 00:16:07.187
everyone's offering four
megabytes in storage,

00:16:07.187 --> 00:16:09.303
we're going to offer like
a gigabyte of storage

00:16:09.303 --> 00:16:11.310
and we'll give people
some amount of storage

00:16:11.310 --> 00:16:14.317
like a gig for free and
then we'll charge them for

00:16:14.317 --> 00:16:15.917
more space.

00:16:15.917 --> 00:16:17.178
Simple, (mumbling).

00:16:17.178 --> 00:16:18.454
And he's like I'm not so sure.

00:16:18.454 --> 00:16:21.681
I think we should maybe
target ads at the email.

00:16:21.681 --> 00:16:24.809
And I was like you know Paul,
that's going to be just creepy.

00:16:24.809 --> 00:16:26.539
So we do say this to each other sometimes.

00:16:26.539 --> 00:16:27.759
I was like, people are going to think

00:16:27.759 --> 00:16:29.027
that everyone's reading their email

00:16:29.027 --> 00:16:30.596
and putting these ads against it.

00:16:30.596 --> 00:16:31.774
That's a really bad idea.

00:16:31.774 --> 00:16:33.285
And we argued and argued
and argued about it.

00:16:33.285 --> 00:16:36.000
I was like Paul, we don't
even have an email program

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:37.496
that can send and receive
email for more than

00:16:37.496 --> 00:16:40.036
the six of us that are using
it right now. (laughing)

00:16:40.036 --> 00:16:41.542
'Cause we had this little program inside.

00:16:41.542 --> 00:16:43.518
And he was like, fine, and
I was like how about we just

00:16:43.518 --> 00:16:46.541
figure out like, how to
get this working before we,

00:16:46.541 --> 00:16:49.337
you know, really go into a lot of detail

00:16:49.337 --> 00:16:51.222
about how we're going
to make money from it?

00:16:51.222 --> 00:16:52.856
And

00:16:52.856 --> 00:16:54.919
Paul and I were both night owls and so

00:16:54.919 --> 00:16:57.105
around three in the
morning I went to leave.

00:16:57.105 --> 00:16:59.308
And I remember walking out the door

00:16:59.308 --> 00:17:00.883
'cause Paul would come
in, he was sort of like

00:17:00.883 --> 00:17:04.485
the typical engineer,
come in about noon and so

00:17:04.485 --> 00:17:06.344
I left at three, Paul
was still working away.

00:17:06.344 --> 00:17:07.546
And I remember walking out the door,

00:17:07.546 --> 00:17:08.816
we had this little glass panel.

00:17:08.816 --> 00:17:10.365
As I walked out I leaned back and I said,

00:17:10.365 --> 00:17:13.554
so Paul, we agreed, we're
not going to explore

00:17:13.554 --> 00:17:16.538
that ads idea, right
now, right? (laughing)

00:17:16.538 --> 00:17:20.251
And Paul was like oh,
yeah yeah, yeah yeah.

00:17:20.251 --> 00:17:22.582
And so Paul stayed for another four hours

00:17:22.582 --> 00:17:26.558
until seven AM when he
went home, building stuff.

00:17:26.558 --> 00:17:27.785
And

00:17:27.785 --> 00:17:29.094
as I said there were
six of us in the company

00:17:29.094 --> 00:17:31.009
using the early form of Gmail.

00:17:31.009 --> 00:17:33.132
And I came back in at
like nine in the morning,

00:17:33.132 --> 00:17:37.299
logged into my email and
there were ads everywhere!

00:17:38.172 --> 00:17:39.830
(laughing)

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:42.698
And so Paul had hooked up this
system that grabbed keywords

00:17:42.698 --> 00:17:45.272
out of your email and
put ads against them.

00:17:45.272 --> 00:17:47.022
And I was like oh no!

00:17:48.256 --> 00:17:50.013
Larry and Sergey are going
to show up at like 11.

00:17:50.013 --> 00:17:51.058
They're going to log into their email,

00:17:51.058 --> 00:17:53.312
there's going to be ads everywhere.

00:17:53.312 --> 00:17:55.121
But I was like you know,
but I could tell from Paul's

00:17:55.121 --> 00:17:56.726
emails to me at the end of the night

00:17:56.726 --> 00:17:58.074
he had been working till at least seven.

00:17:58.074 --> 00:17:59.949
So I was like I don't
want to wake him up now.

00:17:59.949 --> 00:18:01.543
But I'm going to have to
call him at like 10:30

00:18:01.543 --> 00:18:05.710
and be like, you've got to log
in and like, turn this off.

00:18:06.668 --> 00:18:08.481
And so the funny thing was
then I had from like nine

00:18:08.481 --> 00:18:10.630
until 10:30 to kind of play with it.

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:13.735
And Al Gore was coming by the office,

00:18:13.735 --> 00:18:16.203
and there was an ad for Al Gore books.

00:18:16.203 --> 00:18:17.674
And one of my friends
had mailed me and said

00:18:17.674 --> 00:18:19.488
do you want to go for a hike this weekend?

00:18:19.488 --> 00:18:22.235
And there was an ad for hiking boots.

00:18:22.235 --> 00:18:26.628
And I was like you know,
these ads aren't bad.

00:18:26.628 --> 00:18:29.961
They're at least relevant if not useful.

00:18:31.885 --> 00:18:35.762
And so by 10:30 I decided
I wasn't going to call Paul

00:18:35.762 --> 00:18:37.354
and Larry and Sergey came in

00:18:37.354 --> 00:18:38.949
and then this interesting thing happened

00:18:38.949 --> 00:18:41.971
where, and it's sort of
interesting in terms of history

00:18:41.971 --> 00:18:43.229
'cause people get confused.

00:18:43.229 --> 00:18:44.854
You're building Gmail, and I do think that

00:18:44.854 --> 00:18:46.311
whether or not targeting ads at email

00:18:46.311 --> 00:18:48.579
is still an open question, is
still an open issue of debate

00:18:48.579 --> 00:18:51.521
between Paul and I maybe
that's how we do it.

00:18:51.521 --> 00:18:54.235
But what happened that night
was Paul basically figured out

00:18:54.235 --> 00:18:56.600
how to target our text
ads that had been running

00:18:56.600 --> 00:18:59.979
alongside of search at content.

00:18:59.979 --> 00:19:03.581
Which traditionally on
the web, about 5% of

00:19:03.581 --> 00:19:06.203
page views that are done
every day are searches.

00:19:06.203 --> 00:19:08.425
So if you walk into a
cybercafe or a classroom

00:19:08.425 --> 00:19:11.698
about one in 20 screens
will have a search page up.

00:19:11.698 --> 00:19:14.016
Which basically meant we
had a great database of ads

00:19:14.016 --> 00:19:17.446
that was good at making
money from 5% of the web.

00:19:17.446 --> 00:19:20.656
What Paul had done was
given any paragraph,

00:19:20.656 --> 00:19:23.613
given any amount of text,
he could find a relevant ad

00:19:23.613 --> 00:19:26.245
from our database to run alongside it.

00:19:26.245 --> 00:19:29.675
And so it basically was a way
of taking our ads database

00:19:29.675 --> 00:19:30.959
and saying we're not just
going to put these ads

00:19:30.959 --> 00:19:33.619
on Google Search, we're going
to put these ads out on the web.

00:19:33.619 --> 00:19:36.341
So today when you see
those blocks of textual ads

00:19:36.341 --> 00:19:38.556
that say Ads By Google on the bottom,

00:19:38.556 --> 00:19:40.291
that's something called AdSense

00:19:40.291 --> 00:19:42.434
and it was actually born that night.

00:19:42.434 --> 00:19:45.029
And it in truth is actually
much more successful

00:19:45.029 --> 00:19:47.328
than even ads inside of Gmail were.

00:19:47.328 --> 00:19:49.482
'Cause Larry and Sergey
said wow, this is amazing.

00:19:49.482 --> 00:19:52.416
Let's rip this out of Gmail,
just let people run this

00:19:52.416 --> 00:19:54.275
on whatever pages they
have on the web which meant

00:19:54.275 --> 00:19:56.012
that bloggers could
suddenly quit their job

00:19:56.012 --> 00:19:58.071
and blog full time 'cause
they could make money from it.

00:19:58.071 --> 00:20:01.525
And people, like About.com
were like we can put

00:20:01.525 --> 00:20:03.742
even more content online 'cause
we can make money from it.

00:20:03.742 --> 00:20:04.575
And so

00:20:05.573 --> 00:20:08.344
you know we launched AdSense
which I think last year

00:20:08.344 --> 00:20:10.839
was somewhere in the five
to seven billion dollar

00:20:10.839 --> 00:20:12.006
revenue range.

00:20:13.073 --> 00:20:15.586
So it was just this
really amazing thing where

00:20:15.586 --> 00:20:18.365
that Gmail, that experiment
that happened inside of Gmail

00:20:18.365 --> 00:20:20.872
became this big product outside which

00:20:20.872 --> 00:20:23.404
I almost quelched with my hey Paul,

00:20:23.404 --> 00:20:25.951
we agreed we're not doing
that right now right?

00:20:25.951 --> 00:20:27.440
(laughing)

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:29.447
But you know, those types
of things it's just,

00:20:29.447 --> 00:20:31.447
it's that Pasteur quote,

00:20:32.759 --> 00:20:34.871
"chance favors the prepared mind."

00:20:34.871 --> 00:20:36.636
You're messing around
with these types of things

00:20:36.636 --> 00:20:39.787
and seeing potentials
and opportunities and

00:20:39.787 --> 00:20:42.406
whether or not ads in
email is a good idea,

00:20:42.406 --> 00:20:44.543
thinking about how ads
could target at email

00:20:44.543 --> 00:20:47.442
let us take ads and target
it really broadly at content.

